<!-- <img SRC="eqns/ts24.gif" height=188 width=162> -->

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Plot comparing single and double exponential smoothing forecasts
</I>
</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
A plot of these results is very enlightening.
<p>
<center>
<img SRC="gifs/tseries4.gif" height=266 width=400 ALT=
"Plot showing single and double exponential smoothing forecasts">
</center>
<p>
This graph indicates that double smoothing follows the data much
closer than single smoothing.  Furthermore, for forecasting single
smoothing cannot do better than projecting a straight horizontal
line, which is not very likely to occur in reality.  So in this
case double smoothing is preferred.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Plot comparing double exponential smoothing and regression
forecasts
</I>
</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
Finally, let us compare double smoothing with linear regression:
<p>
<center>
<img SRC="gifs/tseries5.gif" height=266 width=400
     ALT="Plot showing raw data with double exponential smoothing
 and regression forecasts">
</center>
<p>
This is an interesting picture.  Both techniques follow the data
in similar fashion, but the regression line is more conservative.
That is, there is a slower increase with the regression line than
with double smoothing.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Selection of technique depends on the forecaster
</I>
</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
The selection of the technique depends on the forecaster.  If it is
desired to portray the growth process in a more aggressive manner,
then one selects double smoothing.  Otherwise, regression may be
more preferable.  It should be noted that in linear regression
"time" functions as the independent variable.
<a href="../../pmd/pmd.htm">Chapter 4</a> discusses the
<a href="../../pmd/section1/pmd141.htm">basics</a> of linear
regression, and the details of regression
<a href="../../pmd/section4/pmd431.htm">estimation.</a>

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

   
</TABLE>

